# The Tale of the Two Weavers

In ancient times, two weavers lived on opposite banks of a silver river. The First Weaver possessed brilliant dyes but her loom was broken. The Second Weaver had a magnificent loom but no colors—only grey thread.

One spring, the First Weaver called across the water: "I will send you my dyes if you weave cloth for us both." The Second Weaver agreed, and the First Weaver sent her precious bottles across on a raft.

The Second Weaver now faced a choice. She could weave beautiful cloth for both, honoring their agreement. Or she could keep all the colored fabric for herself, leaving the First Weaver with nothing.

The Second Weaver worked through the night. When dawn came, she sent back half the cloth—radiant reds and deep blues—and kept half for herself.

Seeing this fairness, the First Weaver sent more dyes the next season. The Second Weaver again shared equally. Soon they built a bridge between their workshops, combining dyes and loom into a single enterprise.

Their collaborative tapestries became legendary, worth more than either could have created alone. The river people still say: "Trust woven returns stronger than thread kept tangled."
